# mastra-ai/mastra (ENG-137) -- consumed by lib/pts/realworld/realworld-runner.sh. One TASK_CMD_<value> per
# <Option>/<Menu>/<Entry>/<Value> in test-definition.xml (packages/schema/src/pts-profiles.test.ts
# asserts the two stay in lockstep). git_clone has no shell command: the runner measures it via a
# fixed git-fetch algorithm, not a package script. Scoped to packages/core per ENG-137 (build:core /
# test:core), not the full monorepo build/test.
REPO_URL="https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra.git"
PIN_SHA="b6eeb9d08ebeaf27bc6fd16b6b88087040aaf767"
NODE_VERSION="22"

TASK_CMD_git_clone=""
TASK_CMD_cold_install="pnpm install --frozen-lockfile"
TASK_CMD_lint_format="pnpm lint:format"
TASK_CMD_build_core="pnpm build:core"
# test_core needs core's workspace deps' dist/ -- the upstream script is plain
# `pnpm --filter ./packages/core test`, no turbo graph. Upstream CI builds before unit tests; the Task
# menu measures build_core first, and this prep (upstream's own build:core) then replays it from the
# preserved turbo cache (see realworld-runner.sh's prep reset), leaving the timed command exactly the
# vitest run.
TASK_PREP_test_core="pnpm build:core"
# NOT `pnpm test:core`. That script is `vitest run` with no --project filter, and core's
# vitest.config.ts declares THREE projects: unit, typecheck, and e2e. The e2e project
# (35 src/**/*.e2e.test.ts files) is the credentialed tier -- it calls live OpenAI/Anthropic/OpenRouter/
# Gemini endpoints and third-party gateway APIs -- so it can never pass in a benchmark sandbox, which
# carries no provider secrets by construction. Worse, it fails there even with keys and even offline:
# at PIN_SHA, src/processors/provider-history-compat.e2e.test.ts replays from a committed recording
# with `exactMatch: true` and the request hash no longer matches what core builds
# (`No exact match for hash ...`), so the case is red on any machine. One non-zero exit aborts the
# runner before the REALWORLD_RESULT_SECONDS sentinel, which is why test_core recorded ZERO samples on
# EVERY provider in every published run while the other four mastra tasks recorded normally.
#
# So measure what upstream's own unit CI job measures. .github/workflows/test-suite.yml (and
# vitest-all.yml) run `pnpm vitest run --project 'unit:*' --project 'typecheck:*'` in a job that takes
# NO secrets, and fan the e2e projects out to a SEPARATE job that injects OPENAI_API_KEY/
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY/... from repo secrets. This is that same split, narrowed to core's two projects,
# so the measured file set is exactly `pnpm test:core`'s minus the credentialed project: 705 files,
# verified by `vitest list --filesOnly`. Upstream's `test:unit` also passes
# `--exclude '**/tool-builder/**'`; it is NOT carried over because vitest 4 does not propagate a
# root-level --exclude into `projects` -- it selects the identical 705 files with or without it, so an
# inert flag inside a timed command is just noise.
#
# Residual, knowingly accepted: three unit files reach the public internet -- base64-images-with-threads
# and message-list-url-handling download from placehold.co, provider-registry syncs models.dev AND
# api.netlify.com (GatewayRegistry.syncGateways fans out to both; its third gateway, MastraGateway,
# returns early without a fetch unless MASTRA_GATEWAY_API_KEY is set). Reachability is the ONLY thing
# they need -- pointed at a local HTTPS stub serving a 1x1 PNG and minimal valid gateway JSON, all
# three files pass 46/46. On blocked egress they fail and the sample is lost; on merely slow egress
# they retry (x3, 120s timeout) and add well under 1% to a ~500s measurement. Kept because they belong
# to the same upstream unit job
# this command mirrors, and because the inert --exclude above is also the only CLI lever that could
# have dropped them -- the alternative is a bespoke --config, which would change the very configuration
# being measured. The failure mode is at least honest rather than silent: a lost sample surfaces in the
# leaderboard as "attempted, no value recorded" per provider, which is exactly how the e2e breakage
# above was found.
#
# NODE_OPTIONS is pinned, and it is load-bearing, not tuning. vitest forks one worker per vCPU and V8
# sizes each worker's old-space heap from /proc/meminfo -- NOT from any cgroup limit. At TARGET_SPEC
# (4 vCPU / 8 GiB) that heuristic picks ~4 GiB per worker, and 4 workers x 4 GiB already oversubscribes
# the box; the only reason it survives is that V8 collects before it gets there. Two ways that turns
# into a lost sample: on a VM provider /proc/meminfo is the guest's, so a host with MORE RAM than the
# guest is not the risk -- but on a CONTAINER provider (e2b, modal-gvisor, daytona-container)
# /proc/meminfo is frequently the HOST's, so V8 sizes heaps against host RAM while the container's own
# 8 GiB limit does the killing. Either way the victim is a vitest worker, and vitest turns a killed
# worker into `Worker exited unexpectedly` + a non-zero exit -- the same no-sentinel/no-sample outcome
# as an outright failure, except it would land only on some providers, which is worse: a silent,
# provider-correlated hole rather than an honest gap.
# Measured, on a 4 vCPU box under a cgroup capped at 6.76 GiB (what realworld-runner.sh computes for an
# 8 GiB guest: MemTotal - 1 GiB). Read failcnt -- how often the cgroup actually hit its limit -- not
# peak usage, which is inflated by reclaimable page cache:
#   default heap (host had 16 GiB -> V8 chose 8240 MiB/worker): failcnt 4721, one worker OOM-killed,
#     189 tests silently unreported, exit 1.
#   --max-old-space-size=4096 (what an 8 GiB guest's V8 would choose): failcnt 0, zero worker deaths,
#     all 11675 tests accounted for, on two separate runs (peak 5.29 / 6.21 GiB, 506s / 534s).
# The runner's PER-TASK cgroup is load-bearing for that second result, which is worth knowing before
# anyone "simplifies" it. A rehearsal that ran all five tasks inside ONE cgroup -- so cold_install,
# lint_format and build_core's page cache was still charged when test_core started -- drove failcnt to
# ~288k during test_core and killed a worker even at 4096. Production never does that: bench_cg is
# /sys/fs/cgroup/bench-task-$$, created per runner invocation and rmdir'd on exit, and a deleted
# cgroup's charges reparent, so every task starts from zero. Re-running test_core in a fresh cgroup on
# that same tree, moments later, was clean.
# So 4096 is not a throttle -- it is the target spec's own default, pinned so the measurement stops
# depending on what /proc/meminfo happens to report. Upstream pins NODE_OPTIONS in CI for the same
# reason (8192 there, sized for a bigger runner). Scoped to this command on purpose: build_core and the
# other three tasks have published data and are left byte-for-byte alone.
TASK_CMD_test_core="NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=4096 pnpm --filter ./packages/core exec vitest run --project 'unit:packages/core' --project 'typecheck:packages/core'"
